1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Three jobs: J1 has the highest priority, J2 the middle, J3 the lowest.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
J3 takes the lock. J1 arrives, asks for it, and is blocked — correctly, since the lock is held.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
J2 then runs its whole 8 units, because it outranks J3 and never needs the lock.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
J1 waits 10 units for a 3-unit section. That is priority inversion.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Lengthen the middle job by one unit and J1's wait grows by one unit: 2 + L.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Add a second middle job and the wait becomes 18. Six middle jobs give 50.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The inversion is unbounded: no constant limits the wait, only the middle work does.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The wait is bounded by the middle work, and the middle work is not part of the argument.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The fix is one rule: while J3 holds a lock that J1 is blocked on, J3 runs at J1's priority.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
J2 can no longer preempt the holder, so J1 waits 2 units instead of 10.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
J3's priority changes twice: up when J1 blocks, back down when the section ends.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Inheritance removes the inversion but keeps the blocking: 2 units is still a wait.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Each semaphore gets a ceiling: the highest priority of any job that may lock it.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A job may enter a section only if its priority is above every ceiling currently locked by others.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
So J1 is blocked on S2 while asking for S1 — a lock it does not want.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Three kinds of blocking: direct, push-through and ceiling.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
J1 needs two locks in sequence. Under inheritance it is blocked twice.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The bound under inheritance is min(n, m) critical sections: here min(2, 2) = 2.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The ceiling protocol gives one episode instead of two.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The same 8 ticks in both, but 1 episode against 2 — the bound improved, not this run.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Two jobs, two locks, opposite order. J1 holds S1 and wants S2; J2 holds S2 and wants S1.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The model deadlocks at time 4 under inheritance: both jobs blocked, neither finished.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The ceiling test refuses the second lock, so the cycle cannot close. All jobs finish.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Over all 121 release-time pairs: 31 deadlocks under inheritance, none under the ceiling.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Mars Pathfinder, 1997: a low-priority weather task held a mutex the bus task needed.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Several medium-priority tasks ran first, so the holder could not release.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The bus task missed its 8 Hz deadline, the watchdog fired, and the spacecraft reset.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The fix was one creation flag: turn priority inheritance on for that mutex.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Inversion: the wait ends, but its length is set by the wrong job.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Deadlock: the wait never ends, because the waits form a cycle.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Starvation: a job is runnable the whole time and never chosen.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
And being blocked by a more urgent job is not inversion at all.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Shape one: given a trace, how long is the high-priority job blocked?

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Shape two: how many blocking episodes, and what is the worst-case bound?

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Shape three: which protocol deadlocks on this program, and which does not?

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Shape four: compute a ceiling. Shape five: state what a protocol guarantees.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Trap 1: priority inversion is not deadlock. The wait here ends.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap 2: inheritance raises the holder, never the waiter.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Trap 3: ceiling blocking is not a bug. A free lock may be refused.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap 4: the ceiling protocol does not always block less. The bound shrinks, not the run.

